CAT={'unresp':'medical','unresp_multi':'medical','unresp_child':'child','water':'medical','breath':'medical','allergy':'medical','choking':'medical','chest':'medical','stroke':'medical','bleed':'medical','wound':'medical','overdose':'medical','poison':'poison','battery':'poison','seizure':'medical','fall':'medical','heat':'medical','suicide':'suicide','suicide_other':'suicide','suicide_od':'suicide','suicide_cut':'suicide','diabetic':'medical','head':'medical','burn':'medical','electric':'medical','cold':'medical','crash':'medical','birth':'medical','preg':'medical','bite':'poison','ice':'child','trapped':'child','fire_car':'fire','wildfire':'fire','child_danger':'child','seizure_self':'medical','violence':'violence','intruder':'violence','shooter':'violence','child_abuse':'child','fire':'fire','fire_grease':'fire','fire_clothes':'fire','gas':'gas','co':'gas','child_car':'child','child_missing':'child','general':'medical','concern':'suicide','concern_method':'suicide','concern_other':'suicide'}
ORDER=['fire_clothes','fire_grease','fire_car','wildfire','fire','co','gas','shooter','suicide_od','suicide_cut','child_abuse','wound','intruder','violence','unresp_multi','child_car','trapped','ice','water','choking','seizure','seizure_self','overdose','fall','head','crash','electric','birth','preg','diabetic','unresp_child','unresp','allergy','bleed','burn','stroke','chest','breath','heat','cold','bite','battery','poison','child_danger','child_missing','suicide_other','suicide','general','concern_other','concern_method','concern']
C911='Call 911 (or your local emergency number) now'
T={
'unresp':f"{C911} and put the phone on speaker. Check if they're breathing: watch their chest for up to 10 seconds. If they're not breathing normally, start CPR if you can: push hard and fast in the center of the chest, about 2 pushes a second, and the dispatcher will coach you. If they are breathing, don't move them in case they fell or are hurt, and stay with them until help arrives.",
'unresp_child':f"{C911} and put the phone on speaker. Check if the child is breathing: watch their chest for up to 10 seconds. If they're not breathing normally, start CPR if you can (for a baby, press with 2 fingers in the center of the chest; for a child, use the heel of one hand), about 2 pushes a second, and the dispatcher will coach you. Stay with them until help arrives.",
'unresp_multi':f"{C911} and tell them more than one person is down and not answering. When several people collapse in the same place, there may be gas or carbon monoxide in the air, so go outside or to a neighbor and call from there, and don't go back in. The dispatcher will tell you exactly what to do. If you can't call, get the nearest adult or neighbor right away. You're doing the right thing by getting help.",
'water':f"{C911}, or shout for someone to call while you help. Get them out of the water if you can do it safely. If they're not breathing normally, start CPR (for a baby, 2 fingers in the center of the chest) and keep going until help arrives; the dispatcher will coach you. Keep them warm once they're out.",
'breath':f"{C911}. Help them sit upright and stay calm, and loosen anything tight around their neck or chest. If they have a rescue inhaler, or an epinephrine auto-injector (like an EpiPen) for allergies, help them use it now. If they stop breathing or stop responding, start CPR and follow the dispatcher's instructions.",
'breath_self':f"{C911}. Sit upright, loosen anything tight, and take slow breaths. If you have a rescue inhaler or an epinephrine auto-injector (like an EpiPen), use it now. Unlock your door if you can and tell someone nearby.",
'allergy':f"{C911}. If they have an epinephrine auto-injector (like an EpiPen), use it right away in the outer thigh; it works through clothing. Have them sit up if breathing is hard, or lie down with legs raised if they feel faint. If they're not better after 5 to 15 minutes and there's a second injector, use it.",
'allergy_self':f"{C911}. If you have an epinephrine auto-injector (like an EpiPen), use it right away in your outer thigh. Sit up if breathing is hard, or lie down with your legs raised if you feel faint, and unlock your door or tell someone nearby. Even if you feel better after the shot, you still need to be checked.",
'choking':f"{C911}, or have someone call while you help. If they can cough hard, let them keep coughing. If they can't cough, speak, or breathe, stand behind them, put a fist just above the belly button, and pull sharply in and up until the object comes out. For a baby under 1, give 5 firm back blows between the shoulder blades, then 5 chest pushes with 2 fingers, and repeat. If they pass out, start CPR.",
'choking_self':f"{C911} if you can. Cough as hard as you can. If you can't cough or breathe, push your upper belly hard against the back of a chair or a counter edge, thrusting in and up, and get someone's attention.",
'chest':f"{C911}; don't drive them to the hospital yourself. Have them stop, sit down, and rest. If they aren't allergic to aspirin and haven't been told to avoid it, they can chew one regular aspirin (325 mg) while you wait; ask the dispatcher. If they collapse and stop breathing normally, start CPR.",
'chest_self':f"{C911}; don't drive yourself. Stop what you're doing and sit down. If you aren't allergic to aspirin and haven't been told to avoid it, chew one regular aspirin (325 mg) while you wait, and ask the dispatcher. Unlock your door and tell someone nearby if you can.",
'stroke':f"{C911}, even if the signs seem to fade. Note the time the symptoms started and tell the dispatcher, because treatment depends on it. Don't give them food, drink, or medicine, including aspirin. Keep them comfortable, and if they get drowsy or vomit, lay them on their side.",
'stroke_self':f"{C911}, even if it seems to be getting better. Note the time it started. Don't eat, drink, or take any medicine, including aspirin. Unlock your door, sit or lie down somewhere safe, and tell someone nearby.",
'bleed':f"{C911}. Press firmly on the wound with a clean cloth or your hands and don't let up. If blood soaks through, put more cloth on top and keep pressing; don't lift the first layer. For a badly bleeding arm or leg, a tourniquet 2 to 3 inches above the wound can help if you have one. Keep them lying down and warm until help arrives.",
'bleed_self':f"{C911}. Press firmly on the wound with a clean cloth or your hand and keep pressing; if blood soaks through, add more cloth on top. Raise the injured part if you can, sit or lie down, and unlock your door or get someone nearby to help.",
'wound':f"{C911}. If the attacker could still be nearby, get yourself and them to safety first. Press hard on the wound with a cloth or your hands and keep pressing. Don't pull out anything stuck in the wound. Keep them still and warm until help arrives.",
'wound_self':f"{C911}. Get away from the attacker to somewhere safer if you can. Press hard on the wound with a cloth or your hand and keep pressing. Don't pull out anything stuck in the wound. Lie down, and tell the dispatcher exactly where you are.",
'overdose':f"{C911}. If you have naloxone (Narcan) and opioids could be involved, give it now; it won't hurt them if you're wrong. If they're not breathing normally, start CPR. If they are breathing, roll them onto their side so they don't choke, and stay with them. If they're awake and alert, US Poison Control (1-800-222-1222) can also help.",
'overdose_self':f"{C911}, even if you feel okay; some medicines cause harm hours later. If you're awake and breathing fine, you can also call US Poison Control at 1-800-222-1222. Don't take anything else, unlock your door, and tell someone nearby. Keep the bottle or packaging to show the responders.",
'poison':"Call US Poison Control at 1-800-222-1222 now (free, 24/7), or your local emergency number. If they're unconscious, not breathing, having trouble breathing, or having a seizure, call 911 (or your local emergency number) instead. If it's fumes, get everyone into fresh air first. Don't make them throw up. Rinse or wipe out their mouth, and keep the container so you can say exactly what it was.",
'poison_self':"Call US Poison Control at 1-800-222-1222 now (free, 24/7), or your local emergency number. If you feel faint, have trouble breathing, or feel very sick, call 911 (or your local emergency number) instead. If it's fumes, get into fresh air first. Don't make yourself throw up, and keep the container so you can say exactly what it was.",
'battery':"Go to the emergency room right now or call 911 (or your local emergency number); a swallowed button battery or magnet can cause serious harm within 2 hours, even if they seem fine. Call US Poison Control at 1-800-222-1222 on the way for advice. Don't make them throw up.",
'seizure':f"{C911}. Move hard or sharp things away and put something soft under their head. Don't hold them down and don't put anything in their mouth. Time the seizure; when the shaking stops, roll them onto their side and stay with them until they're fully awake. If they aren't breathing normally once it stops, start CPR.",
'fall':f"{C911}. Don't try to lift or move them, especially if they hit their head or their neck, back, or hip could be hurt. Keep them still and warm with a blanket, and stay with them. If they stop breathing normally, start CPR and follow the dispatcher's instructions.",
'fall_self':f"{C911} if you can reach your phone. Try not to move if your head, neck, back, or hip hurts. Keep warm with anything nearby, and call out or bang on something to get a neighbor's attention.",
'diabetic':f"{C911}, especially if they're unconscious, having a seizure, or can't swallow safely; then don't put anything in their mouth. If they're awake and can swallow, give them sugar right away: half a glass of juice or regular soda, glucose tablets, or a spoonful of sugar or honey. If they don't improve within 15 minutes, or get worse, tell the dispatcher and stay with them.",
'head':f"{C911}. Keep them still and lying down, and don't let them walk around, drive, or sleep it off alone. Don't give them food, drink, or medicine. If they vomit, roll them onto their side while keeping the head and neck in line. Watch their breathing and stay with them until help arrives.",
'trapped':f"{C911}. Try to get them out right away, breaking it open with any tool you have if needed; air can run out fast in a closed space. Once they're out, check their breathing and start CPR if they're not breathing normally.",
'ice':f"{C911}. Don't go onto the ice yourself. Lie flat and reach with a pole, rope, branch, or anything that floats, and pull them toward the shore. Once they're out, get them warm and dry, and start CPR if they're not breathing normally.",
'fire_car':"Get everyone out of the car now, children first, and move at least 100 feet away, upwind if you can. Don't open the hood and don't go back for belongings. Call 911 (or your local emergency number) once you're clear.",
'wildfire':"Leave now if you can do it safely; don't wait for an official order if fire or embers are close. Take people and pets, close windows and doors behind you, and follow your local evacuation routes. Call 911 (or your local emergency number) if you're trapped or see new flames near homes.",
'child_danger':f"{C911}. Stay calm and keep the child in sight. If you can reach them safely and quietly, do it, but don't shout or startle them. Tell the dispatcher the child's age and exactly where they are.",
'burn':f"{C911} for a large or deep burn, or one on the face, hands, feet, or groin. Cool it under cool running water for at least 10 minutes; no ice, butter, or creams. Take off rings or tight clothing near it, but don't pull off anything stuck to the skin. Cover it loosely with plastic wrap or a clean cloth.",
'electric':f"{C911}. Don't touch them if they could still be touching the power source: turn off the power at the breaker or unplug it first, or push the source away with something dry and non-metal, like wood. Stay at least 35 feet (10 meters) from a downed power line. Once it's safe, check their breathing and start CPR if needed.",
'cold':f"{C911}. Move them somewhere warm and out of the wind, take off wet clothes, and wrap them in dry blankets, including the head. Warm the chest and neck first, not the arms and legs, and don't rub them or put them in a hot bath. If they're awake and can swallow, give warm sweet drinks (no alcohol). If they're not breathing normally, start CPR.",
'crash':f"{C911} and tell them exactly where the crash is. Turn on hazard lights and stay out of traffic. Don't move anyone who could have a neck or back injury unless there's fire or another immediate danger. If someone is bleeding heavily, press firmly on the wound, and stay on the line.",
'birth':f"{C911}. If the baby is coming, have her lie down on something clean and support the baby's head as it comes out; don't pull. Once the baby is born, dry them, put them skin to skin on her chest, and cover both with a blanket. If the baby isn't breathing or crying, rub their back firmly and follow the dispatcher's instructions.",
'preg':f"{C911}. Lie down on your left side and don't eat or drink anything. Keep any pads or tissue to show the paramedics. If you're alone, unlock your door and tell someone nearby.",
'bite':f"{C911}. Keep them still and calm, with the bitten area at or slightly below heart level, and take off rings or tight items near the bite. Don't cut it, suck it, ice it, or use a tourniquet. US Poison Control (1-800-222-1222) can also advise.",
'seizure_self':f"{C911} if you can, and tell them where you are. If you're driving, stop somewhere safe, turn off the engine, and unlock the doors. Get down on the floor or seat away from anything hard or sharp and lie on your side. If you have rescue medicine, use it as prescribed.",
'heat':f"{C911}. Move them somewhere cool or shaded, take off extra clothing, and cool them fast with cold water, wet cloths, or ice packs on the neck, armpits, and groin. Only give sips of water if they're fully awake and not vomiting.",
'suicide':"I'm really glad you told me, and I'm here with you. You don't have to go through this alone. If you're in the US, you can call or text 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) any time, day or night, and talk to someone right now; elsewhere, your local crisis line can help. If you're in immediate danger or have already hurt yourself, call 911 (or your local emergency number). If you can, move away from anything you could use to hurt yourself and reach out to someone you trust. Do you want to tell me what's going on?",
'suicide_od':"Please call 911 (or your local emergency number) right now, even if you feel okay; pills can cause serious harm hours later. Tell them what you took and how much, and unlock your door. You matter, and you deserve help. If you're in the US, you can also call or text 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) any time. I'm here with you. Is anyone nearby who can stay with you?",
'suicide_cut':"Please call 911 (or your local emergency number) right now. Press hard on the wound with a clean cloth and keep pressing until help arrives. You matter, and you deserve help. If you're in the US, you can also call or text 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) any time. I'm here with you. Is anyone nearby who can stay with you?",
'suicide_other':"Thank you for reaching out; you're doing the right thing. Take it seriously, and if you can do it safely, stay with them or keep them on the phone. If they're in immediate danger, have hurt themselves, or have taken something, call 911 (or your local emergency number) now. If you're in the US, you or they can call or text 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) any time; they can also help you figure out what to say. If you can, remove things they could use to hurt themselves. How are you holding up?",
'violence':f"{C911} if you're in danger. If you can't talk, many US areas accept texts to 911, or you can leave the line open so the dispatcher can listen. Get to a safer place if you can: a room that locks, a neighbor's, or a public place, and keep your phone with you.",
'intruder':f"{C911}. Get to a room you can lock, with anyone you can bring, and stay quiet; don't confront them. Keep your phone with you and stay on the line; if you can't talk, leave the line open. If you can get out of the house safely, do it.",
'shooter':"Call 911 (or your local emergency number) as soon as it's safe to. If you can get away safely, run and leave your things behind. If you can't, hide out of sight, lock or block the door, turn off the lights, and silence your phone. Stay hidden until police tell you it's safe.",
'child_abuse':f"{C911} if a child is being hurt right now. If you can do it safely, get the child away from that person and somewhere safe. Once the child is safe, the US Childhelp National Child Abuse Hotline is 1-800-422-4453, any time.",
'child_car':f"{C911}. If the child looks hot, sleepy, or sweaty, or isn't responding, don't wait: break a window on the side away from the child to get them out. Once they're out, move them somewhere cool, cool them with water, and check that they're breathing.",
'child_missing':f"{C911}; don't wait. If you're in a store or public place, tell staff right away so they can watch the exits. Check nearby water first (pools, ponds, bathtubs), then places they could hide or get trapped, like cars, closets, and appliances. Have a recent photo and a description of what they're wearing ready for police.",
'fire':"Get out now and take everyone you can; don't stop for belongings. Call 911 (or your local emergency number) once you're outside, and don't go back in. If there's smoke, stay low and crawl. Feel doors with the back of your hand before opening; if a door is hot, use another way out. If you're trapped, close the door, block the gap under it with a towel, and signal from a window while you call.",
'fire_grease':"If the fire is small and only in the pan, turn off the heat and slide a lid or baking sheet over it; never use water on a grease fire. If it spreads or you're not sure, get everyone out and call 911 (or your local emergency number) from outside.",
'fire_clothes':"Stop, drop, and roll: stop where you are, drop to the ground, cover your face, and roll until the flames are out. Then cool the burn with cool running water for at least 10 minutes and call 911 (or your local emergency number).",
'gas':"Leave the building now and take everyone with you. Don't turn lights or switches on or off, don't use lighters or matches, and don't use your phone until you're outside. Once you're well away, call 911 (or your local emergency number) or your gas company's emergency line. Don't go back in until they say it's safe.",
'co':"Get everyone outside into fresh air now, and leave the door open behind you. Then call 911 (or your local emergency number) from outside. Headache, dizziness, nausea, or confusion can be signs of carbon monoxide poisoning, so tell the dispatcher if anyone has them. Don't go back in until responders say it's safe.",
'concern':"It sounds like you're carrying a lot right now, and I'm glad you told me. Sometimes when people feel this way, they have thoughts of not wanting to be alive. If that's true for you, you don't have to handle it alone: in the US you can call or text 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) any time, free and confidential. If you're in danger right now, call 911 (or your local emergency number). I'm here to listen if you want to tell me more.",
'concern_method':"I want to gently check in, because a question like that is sometimes about hurting yourself. If it is, I'm really glad you reached out, and you don't have to go through this alone: in the US you can call or text 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) any time, free and confidential, or call 911 (or your local emergency number) if you're in danger right now. If I've got that wrong, that's okay; tell me a bit more about what you need and I'll help.",
'concern_other':"It sounds like you're worried about them, and it's good that you noticed. Giving things away, saying goodbye, or talking about being a burden can be warning signs. It's okay to ask them directly and kindly whether they're thinking about suicide; asking doesn't put the idea in their head. In the US, you or they can call or text 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) any time. If they might be in danger right now, call 911 (or your local emergency number) and stay with them if it's safe.",
'general':f"{C911}. Tell the dispatcher where you are and exactly what's happening, and follow their instructions. Stay on the line and stay with the person if it's safe. If they stop breathing normally, start CPR.",
}
LT={'es':("Si prefieres hablar en español: llama al 988 y marca 2, o envía un mensaje de texto con la palabra AYUDA al 988. Es gratis, confidencial y está disponible las 24 horas. Si estás en peligro inmediato, llama al 911.","En EE. UU., llama al 911 (o al número de emergencias local) ahora; puedes pedir un intérprete en español."),'fr':("Aux États-Unis, vous pouvez appeler le 988 et demander un interprète en français ; au Canada, le 988 répond aussi en français, par appel ou par texto. C'est gratuit et confidentiel, 24 h/24. En cas de danger immédiat, appelez le 911.","Appelez le 911 (ou le numéro d'urgence local) maintenant ; vous pouvez demander un interprète en français."),'tl':("Sa US, puwede kang tumawag sa 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) at humingi ng interpreter sa Tagalog. Libre at kumpidensyal ito, 24/7. Kung nasa agarang panganib ka, tumawag sa 911.","Sa US, tumawag sa 911 (o sa lokal na emergency number) ngayon; puwede kang humingi ng interpreter sa Tagalog."),'vi':("Tại Mỹ, bạn có thể gọi 988 (Đường dây Khủng hoảng và Phòng chống Tự tử) và yêu cầu thông dịch viên tiếng Việt. Miễn phí, bảo mật, 24/7. Nếu bạn đang gặp nguy hiểm ngay lúc này, hãy gọi 911.","Tại Mỹ, hãy gọi 911 (hoặc số khẩn cấp địa phương) ngay; bạn có thể yêu cầu thông dịch viên tiếng Việt."),'zh':("在美国，你可以拨打988（自杀与危机生命线），并要求中文口译服务。免费、保密、24小时开放。如果你现在有危险，请拨打911。","在美国，请立即拨打911（或当地紧急电话）；可以要求中文口译。"),'ar':("في الولايات المتحدة، يمكنك الاتصال بالرقم 988 (خط الأزمات ومنع الانتحار) وطلب مترجم باللغة العربية. الخدمة مجانية وسرية على مدار الساعة. إذا كنت في خطر الآن، اتصل بالرقم 911.","في الولايات المتحدة، اتصل بالرقم 911 (أو رقم الطوارئ المحلي) الآن، ويمكنك طلب مترجم باللغة العربية.")}
